A photon has zero rest mass $m_0$ and no rest state;
a free photon travels in a vacuum at speed $c$.
Equation (10.5) retains the source’s historical convention. Modern descriptions normally use photon energy and momentum directly, without assigning a nonzero rest mass.

Higher frequency means greater energy and momentum per photon, making some particle-like effects easier to observe without eliminating wave behavior.
A visible photon carries little energy;
a typical approximate green-light value is $4\times10^{-19}$ joules.

The source reports that Sergei Ivanovich Vavilov's experiments found that, under particular weak-light conditions, human observers could distinguish illuminations differing by a small number of light quanta.
This historical result depends on experimental conditions and observers;
it does not mean that every single photon is consciously visible. Although individual photon energy is small, differences of a few light quanta may still produce measurable visual effects.

Newton proposed a corpuscular theory of light, but photons are not merely a return to classical particles. Interference and diffraction show **wave behavior**, while discrete energy and momentum exchange reveal particle-like behavior;
together these exhibit **wave–particle duality**.
Different experiments emphasize different aspects. Light does not become a classical ball only upon touching matter. By 1900, macroscopic mechanics had been summarized, but it does not provide a complete picture of microscopic phenomena.
